.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-begin\-time=yr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc \-Byr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc
Sets the starting time for data allowed in the input data; pings
with times before the starting time will be ignored.
Default: \fIyr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc\fP = 1962/2/21/10/30/0.
.TP
.B \-\-browse \-D
Starts up the program in "browse" mode. If a file is opened
in browse mode (either at startup or later), none
of the edited data will be output to a file. The default
is to output the edited data to a file.
.TP
.B \-\-end\-time=yr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc \-Eyr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc
Sets the ending time for data allowed in the input data; pings
with times after the ending time will be ignored.
Default: \fIyr/mo/da/hr/mn/sc\fP = 2062/2/21/10/30/0.
.TP
.B \-\-format=format_id \-Fformat_id
Sets the format at startup for the input and output swath sonar data using
\fBMBIO\fP integer format identifiers. This value can also be set
interactively when specifying the input file.
This program uses the \fBMBIO\fP library and will read or write any swath sonar
format supported by \fBMBIO\fP. A list of the swath sonar data formats
currently supported by \fBMBIO\fP and their identifier values
is given in the \fBMBIO\fP manual page.
By default the \fIformat\fP id value is inferred using the \fBMB-System\fP
convention of an ".mbXX" suffix (the XX corresponds to the \fBMBIO\fP format id).
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-mode \-G
This flag causes \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP to treat the \fBDone\fP button as
equivalent to the \fBQuit\fP button. This option is used when
\fBMBnavedit-qt\fP is started automatically by some other process and
only a single file is to edited.
.TP
.B \-\-help \-H
This "help" flag cause the program to print out a description
of its operation and then exit immediately.
.TP
.B \-\-input=file \-Ifile
Sets the data file from which the input data will be read at startup.
This value can also be set interactively.
.TP
.B \-\-strip\-comments \-N
Normally, all \fBMB-System\fP processing
programs embed comment records describing the processing actions
and parameters into output swath data files.
This flag, when used in conjunction with the \fB\-X\fP flag,
causes \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP to invoke \fBmbprocess\fP with it's
\fB\-N\fP option. In turn, this causes \fBmbprocess\fP to
generate an output, processed swath data file without any
comment records.
.TP
.B \-\-use\-ping\-data \-P
Causes \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP to load and work with navigation from
survey data records even if the data format uses asynchronous
navigation records. In this case, the only edits which will
actually be applied to the output data are changes to the
time stamps of the survey records.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose \-V
Normally, \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP outputs information to the stderr stream
regarding the number of records loaded and dumped.  If the
\fB\-V\fP flag is given, then \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP works in a "verbose" mode and
outputs the program version being used, all error status messages,
and a large amount of other information including all of the
beams flagged or zeroed.
.TP
.B \-\-run\-mbprocess \-X
This option causes \fBmbprocess\fP to run automatically
on an input swath data file when the \fBMBnavedit-qt\fP editing session
is completed. The \fB\-X\fP option effectively means that the
corresponding processed swath data is generated or updated immediately.
The program \fBmbprocess\fP will not be invoked if the
editing session is in the browse mode.

.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
.TP
.B File menu
\fBOpen\fP asks for a swath file or datalist. The format is taken from the name of the file, or asked
for when that is not possible. \fBFile Selection List\fP shows the files given to the program,
each marked \fB<loaded>\fP or \fB<Locked>\fP, and \fB<nve>\fP if it has edited navigation; a selected file can be edited or
removed from the list. \fBSave\fP writes the edits once the whole file has been read.
\fBBrowse Only\fP means that the next file opened will not be written.
.TP
.B Edit menu
\fBUndo\fP and \fBRedo\fP. Undo and redo reach back to the last Next Buffer.
.TP
.B View menu
Turns on and off each plot, the original values in each plot, the model in the longitude and latitude
plots, and speed made good and course made good. Roll, pitch and heave are shown together.
.TP
.B Controls menu
\fBTime Stepping\fP sets the time span shown and the time step, in seconds; a slider
doubles or halves its range at its ends.
\fBNav Modeling\fP selects Off, Gaussian Mean, Dead Reckoning or Inversion, and sets the width of the Gaussian
window, the drift rates in longitude and latitude and the weights
against speed and acceleration (with \fBApply\fP).
\fBTime Interpolation\fP and \fBDelete Bad Times\fP treat all repeated or reversed time stamps of the buffer, and can
be applied only when such time stamps have been found.
\fBPosition Offset\fP adds a longitude and latitude offset to the records in memory and
to those read later.
\fBDespike Sonar Depth\fP selects the sonar depths in view that are further than a threshold in meters from
the median of the records within a time window around them. Their squares turn
red, and the largest distance from the median in view is reported to help in choosing the threshold.
\fBInterpolate\fP replaces them and hides the line of original sonar depths, which would otherwise still show the spikes and
keep the vertical range wide. A spike must be shorter than half of the window.
.TP
.B Start, Reverse, Forward, End
Move to the start of the buffer, back or forward by the time step, or to the end of the buffer.
.TP
.B Show All, Pick Zoom
\fBShow All\fP shows the whole buffer. After \fBPick Zoom\fP the left mouse button sets one
edge of the time interval to be shown and the middle button, or a second left click, the other edge; the
right button applies them.
.TP
.B Pick, Select, Deselect, Select All, Deselect All
The edit modes, described under \fBMOUSE ACTIONS\fP.
.TP
.B Next Buffer
Writes all but the end of the buffer and reads more of the file.
.TP
.B Done / Next File
Writes the edits without asking, closes the file, and opens the next file of the list if there is one.
.TP
.B Quit
Closes the program, asking first if there are unsaved edits.
.TP
.B Interpolate, Interpolate Repeats
\fBInterpolate\fP replaces selected values by values interpolated in time
from their nearest unselected neighbours. \fBInterpolate Repeats\fP does this only for selected values which repeat the previous one.
.TP
.B Revert
Returns selected values to their original values.
.TP
.B Flag, Unflag
Marks or unmarks the selected positions as bad, so that the models leave them out.
.TP
.B Use Solution, Use Speed-Made-Good, Use Course-Made-Good
Replace selected longitudes and latitudes by the model, selected speeds by the speed
made good, and selected headings by the course made good.
.TP
.B Slider
Moves through the buffer, one record per wheel notch.

.SH MOUSE ACTIONS
In the \fBPick\fP mode the left button turns on or off the selection of the nearest point within 50 pixels.
In the \fBSelect\fP and \fBDeselect\fP modes the left button changes the points within 10 pixels, and follows the pointer while it is held.
\fBSelect All\fP and \fBDeselect All\fP act on all points in view of the plot clicked and of all plots, respectively.
Selecting in one plot clears selections in the others, and leaving the view clears a selection.
The keys Y and Q choose Pick, U and W Select, I and E Deselect, O and R Select All, and P and T Deselect All.
The middle button moves back and the right button moves forward by the time step.
When all of the plots fit in the window the mouse wheel does the same; otherwise it moves the plots up and down.

